<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.4.4: Factoring Polynomials by Grouping</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01631</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.4.4: Factoring Polynomials by Grouping</md:title>
    <md:uuid>13a4cf5e-47c9-401b-a28f-4c84e491dad2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity prompts students to find a way to factor a polynomial in which there is not a common factor in all of
  the terms of the polynomial. By grouping different parts of the polynomial together, they can find a GCF for each
  individual part. If the polynomial can be factored, a common factor will emerge from both parts.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Start the lesson by explaining that some polynomials will not have a common factor in all of their terms. Use the
  polynomial in the first example to illustrate this point, \(ms − 4m + 7s − 28\). Challenge students to
  find any common factor in all the terms of the polynomial.</para>
<para>Explain that polynomials can be split into smaller parts, and we can start factoring there. Review the example step
  by step. Check for understanding after each step.</para>
<para>Finally, ask students to complete questions 1 – 8 individually. If needed, solve the first one together as a
  class.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>If there is no common term in all of the terms of a polynomial, it may be possible to factor by grouping. To do this,
  separate the polynomial into two parts with two terms in each part. Then look for the GCF in each part. If the
  polynomial can be factored, you will find that a common factor emerges from both parts.</para>
<para>Let’s look at an example. Factor the following polynomial.</para>
<para>\(ms − 4m + 7s − 28\)</para>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Group terms with common factors.</para>
<para>  Is there a GCF of all four terms? No, so let’s separate the first two terms from the second two.</para>
<para>  \(ms − 4m + 7s − 28\)<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1b9de1f635412506c067211646613e19a4b113db"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor in each group.</para>
<para>  Factor the GCF from the first two terms. Factor the GCF from the second two terms.</para>
<para>  \(m(s − 4) + 7s − 28\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor the common factor from the expression.</para>
<para>  Notice that each term has a common factor of \((s − 4)\). Factor out the common factor.</para>
<para>  \(m
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}s} \;{\style{color:red}-}\;     {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red})}
  + 7{\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}s}\;     {\style{color:red}-}\;     {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red})}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check.</para>
<para>  Multiply \((s − 4)(m + 7)\). Is the product the original expression?</para>
<para>  \((s − 4)(m + 7)\)</para>
<para>Work on the following exercises individually.</para>
<para>Factor by grouping:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(xy + 7y + 8x + 56\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x + 7)(y + 8)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(x^2 + 2x − 5x − 10\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para>You can use the following video if you need some help</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b204443db93c94ab3591290f6f435f5498fbd228">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8b34c3c7ea2318bc967925932e4957127ac286c6" srclang="en_us">
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x − 5)(x + 2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(20x^2 − 16x − 15x + 12\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((5x − 4)(4x − 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(x^2 + 4x − 7x − 28\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x + 4)(x − 7)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(2x^2 − 14x − 5x + 35\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x − 7)(2x − 5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(xy − 6x − 4y + 24\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((y − 6)(x − 4)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(9x^2 + 12x − 15x − 20\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((3x − 5)(3x + 4)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(xy − 9x + 2y − 18\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x + 2)(y − 9)\)</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Emphasize to students that if the first two terms do not have a common factor, that does not guarantee that the
  polynomial cannot be factored by grouping. They should still look for a GCF in the last two terms. It might also be
  possible to rearrange the terms so that the polynomial can be factored by grouping. Students should check all
  possibilities before determining that a polynomial is prime.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to write down the steps used when factoring by grouping. Use the list below to guide students as needed:
</para>
<note>
<para> <emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Group terms with common factors.</para>
<para> <emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor in each group. </para>
<para> <emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor from the expression. </para>
<para> <emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check your answer by multiplying the factors together. </para>
</note>
<para>Ask students how many unique ways there are to group a polynomial with four terms into two pairs of terms.</para>
<para>There are 3 ways:</para>
<list>
  <item> Grouping the first two terms together</item>
  <item> Grouping the first and third terms together</item>
  <item> Grouping the first and last terms together</item>
</list>
<title>Video: Factoring by Grouping</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to factor by grouping.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/93677f51e4bf0e85e72e43e92ec87173fbced0a1">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cb519836bf0951ce50170e8eadf4589062c8bda6" srclang="en_us">
      https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/93677f51e4bf0e85e72e43e92ec87173fbced0a1
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>
<title>6.4.4: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Factor by grouping: \(42m^2 − 18m − 35m + 15\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Answers
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Feedback
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((14m − 3)(3m − 5)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Factor out \(6m\) from the first two terms and −5 from
        the second two terms. The correct answer is \((7m − 3)(6m − 5)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((7m − 3)(6m − 5)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiply \((7m − 3)(6m − 5)\) to see if it is equal to \(42m^2
        − 18m − 35m + 15\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((7m + 3)(6m + 5)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Check the signs of each factor. Factor out \(6m\) from the
        first two terms and −5 from the second two terms. The correct answer is \((7m − 3)(6m − 5)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \((7m − 5)(6m − 3)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Factor out \(6m\) from the first two terms and −5 from
        the second two terms. Be careful to match the second term of each binomial with the correct first term. The
        correct answer is \((7m − 3)(6m − 5)\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>6.4.4: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Factoring Polynomials by Grouping</title>
<para>Sometimes there is no common factor of all the terms of a polynomial. When there are four terms, we separate the
  polynomial into two parts with two terms in each part. Then look for the GCF in each part. If the polynomial can be
  factored, you will find that a common factor emerges from both parts. Not all polynomials can be factored. Just like
  some numbers are prime, some polynomials are prime.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>Factor by grouping: \(xy + 3y + 2x + 6\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Group terms with common factors.</para>
<para>  Is there a greatest common factor of all four terms?</para>
<para>  No, so let’s separate the first two terms from the second two.<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9011d6bbc6c3c6974d2c0c8ac9069702c809f4eb"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor in each group.</para>
<para>  Factor the GCF from the first two terms.<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/493b1ccc9b35b8cfcfb1bb807f17b12667eacfc1"/></media></figure></para>
<para>  Factor the GCF from the second two terms.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor the common factor from the expression. </para>
<para>  Notice that each term has a common factor of \((x+3)\).</para>
<para>  \(y {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}x} \;{\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}3}
  {\style{color:red})} + 2 {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}x}\;     {\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}3}
  {\style{color:red})}
  \)</para>
<para>  Factor out the common factor.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check.</para>
<para>  Multiply \((x + 3)(y + 2)\). Is the product the original expression?</para>
<para>  \((x + 3)(y + 2)\) </para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">How to factor by grouping:</para>
<note>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Group terms with common factors.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor in each group.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Factor the common factor from the expression.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
</note>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Factor by grouping: \(x^2 + 3x − 2x − 6\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>There is no GCF in all four terms.</para>
<para>  \(x^2 + 3x − 2x − 6\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Separate into two parts.</para>
<para>  \(x^2 + 3x \;\;\;\;\;\;\; − 2x − 6\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor the GCF from both parts. Be careful with the signs when factoring the GCF from the last two terms.</para>
<para>  \(x(x + 3) − 2(x + 3)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor.</para>
<para>  \((x + 3)(x − 2)\)</para>
<para>Step 5 - 
  Check on your own by multiplying.</para>
<title>Try It: Factoring Polynomials by Grouping</title>
<para>Factor by grouping:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(xy + 8y + 3x + 24\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para>\(6x^2 − 3x − 4x + 2\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to factor polynomials by grouping.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>There is no GCF in all four terms.</para>
    <para>      \(xy + 8y + 3x + 24\)<emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Separate into two parts.</para>
    <para>      \(xy + 8y\;\;\;\;\;\;\;+ 3x + 24\)<emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor the GCF from both parts.</para>
    <para>      \(y(x + 8) + 3(x + 8)\)<emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor.</para>
    <para>      \((x + 8)(y + 3)\)<emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check on your own by multiplying. </para>
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>There is no GCF in all four terms.</para>
    <para>      \(6x^2 − 3x − 4x + 2\)<emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Separate into two parts.</para>
    <para>      \(6x^2 − 3x\;\;\;\;\;\;\;− 4x + 2\)<emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Factor the GCF from both parts.</para>
    <para>      \(3x(2x − 1) − 2(2x − 1)\)<emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Factor out the common factor.</para>
    <para>      \((2x − 1)(3x − 2)\)<emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check on your own by multiplying. </para>
  </item>
</list></section>
</content>
</document>
